    "1241232": {
      "findings": [
        {
          "version": "6.0.10",
          "paths": [
            "artifacts__gxeon-dashboard>@tailwindcss/typography>postcss-selector-parser"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "found_by": null,
      "deleted": null,
      "references": "- https://github.com/postcss/postcss-selector-parser/security/advisories/GHSA-rj75-hqrm-r3gf\n- https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-104844\n- https://github.com/postcss/postcss-selector-parser/commit/62b191792df0a0bc56062e5a875bc74aae2a51cd\n- https://github.com/postcss/postcss-selector-parser/releases/tag/7.1.6\n- https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-rj75-hqrm-r3gf",
      "created": "2026-10-05T22:53:54.000Z",
      "id": 1241232,
      "npm_advisory_id": null,
      "overview": "### Impact\n\n`.` and `#` are not word delimiters in the tokenizer, so a flat selector such as\n`.a.a.a...` reaches `splitWord()` as a single word token carrying n class or id\nindexes. Three passes scanned those index arrays linearly for every index,\nmaking the parse O(n^2) in the number of indexes rather than in input length:\n`uniqs()`, the `indices.forEach` loop, and the Sass-interpolation filter.\nParsing a 400 KB flat selector took ~34 s on a modern laptop, fully occupying a\nsingle thread. A benign selector of identical byte size parses in tens of\nmilliseconds, so the cost is driven by the index count, not the input size.\nThe nesting depth of such a selector is 0, so the `maxNestingDepth` guard added\nin 7.1.3 offers no protection.\n\nReachability is deployment dependent. Only consumers that parse untrusted,\nattacker-supplied selectors synchronously in a request path are exposed, for\nexample CSS sanitizers, CSS-in-JS services and online playgrounds. Ordinary\nbuild-time use on trusted sources is not affected.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in 7.1.6. The three passes now use Set membership tests, making parsing\nlinear in the number of indexes. There is no behaviour change: parsing is\nbyte-identical on a differential corpus of 8413 selectors.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nCap the size of selectors accepted from untrusted sources before parsing.",
      "reported_by": null,
      "title": "PostCSS: Quadratic complexity in flat selector parsing allows CPU exhaustion",
      "metadata": null,
      "cves": [
        "CVE-2026-104844"
      ],
      "access": "public",
      "severity": "moderate",
      "module_name": "postcss-selector-parser",
      "vulnerable_versions": "<7.1.6",
      "github_advisory_id": "GHSA-rj75-hqrm-r3gf",
      "recommendation": "Upgrade to version 7.1.6 or later",
      "patched_versions": ">=7.1.6",
      "updated": "2026-10-05T22:53:57.000Z",
      "cvss": {
        "score": 5.9,
        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H"
      },
      "cwe": [
        "CWE-400",
        "CWE-407"
      ],
      "url": "https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-rj75-hqrm-r3gf"
    },
